Executive summary
The H-E-S-M framework—Hormonal/developmental signaling, Environmental context, State transition, and Nutrient matrix—is a structured comparative architecture for identifying recurring regulatory patterns across plant biotechnology, human nutrition, tissue engineering, and controlled ecological systems.
Its central claim is not that plants and humans are biologically interchangeable. Rather, H-E-S-M proposes that distinct biological systems can display the same high-level operational sequence:
Activation→Specification/Stabilization→Storage/Integration→System maintenance\text{Activation} \rightarrow \text{Specification/Stabilization} \rightarrow \text{Storage/Integration} \rightarrow \text{System maintenance}Activation→Specification/Stabilization→Storage/Integration→System maintenance
Loop One applies this sequence to four independently established domains: wound-induced organogenesis in apple, haploid/doubled-haploid stabilization in citrus, storage-root development in sweet potato, and complete wheat life-cycle production in space-based biological life-support systems. Each stage is supported by domain-specific research; the loop itself is presented as a cross-domain functional isomorphism, meaning that the relational structure of the stages is comparable even though the underlying molecules, tissues, and organisms differ.pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih+2
The framework is therefore a hypothesis-generation and integration tool. It is strongest when used to organize evidence, identify testable correspondences, and prevent false cross-domain extrapolation—not when used to claim direct clinical equivalence between plant culture conditions and human therapy.

1. Purpose and scope
Modern biology often advances by separating questions into specialized silos: plant tissue culture, crop physiology, human nutrition, biomedical engineering, pharmacognosy, and space life support. That specialization is productive, but it can obscure recurring organizational principles that appear across systems.
H-E-S-M is designed to make those recurring principles explicit without collapsing the boundaries between kingdoms or clinical disciplines.
The framework has three objectives:
Describe recurring regulatory and logistical patterns across plant and human biological systems.
Compare those patterns using defined variables rather than loose metaphor.
Generate falsifiable hypotheses that can be tested independently within each domain before any cross-domain inference is accepted.
This approach follows established principles of analogical reasoning in science: analogies are most useful when they map relations among components, not merely surface similarities; and they must remain open to empirical challenge.

